WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.450 --> 00:00:04.569
Dear Fellow Scholars, this is Two Minute Papers
with Károly Zsolnai-Fehér.

00:00:04.569 --> 00:00:06.799
This one is going to be a treat.

00:00:06.799 --> 00:00:11.730
As you know all too well after watching at
least a few episodes of this series, neural

00:00:11.730 --> 00:00:16.480
networks offer us amazingly powerful tools
to defeat problems that we didn't stand a

00:00:16.480 --> 00:00:19.040
chance against for a long long time.

00:00:19.040 --> 00:00:24.390
We are now in the golden age of AI and no
business or field of science is going to remain

00:00:24.390 --> 00:00:26.630
unaffected by this revolution.

00:00:26.630 --> 00:00:31.730
However, this approach comes with its own
disadvantage compared to previous handcrafted

00:00:31.730 --> 00:00:36.060
algorithms, it is harder to know what is really
happening under the hood.

00:00:36.060 --> 00:00:40.980
That's also kind of the advantage of neural
networks, because they can deal with complexities

00:00:40.980 --> 00:00:44.010
that we, humans are not built to comprehend.

00:00:44.010 --> 00:00:49.420
But still, it is always nice to peek within
a neural network and see if it is trying to

00:00:49.420 --> 00:00:53.430
learn the correct concepts that are relevant
to our application.

00:00:53.430 --> 00:00:57.420
Maybe later we'll be able to take a look into
a neural network, learn what it is trying

00:00:57.420 --> 00:01:03.530
to do, simplify it and create a more reliable,
handcrafted algorithm that mimics it.

00:01:03.530 --> 00:01:08.439
What's even more, maybe they will be able
to write this piece of code by themselves.

00:01:08.439 --> 00:01:13.439
So clearly, there is lots of value to be had
from the visualizations, however, this topic

00:01:13.439 --> 00:01:16.729
is way more complex than one would think at
first.

00:01:16.729 --> 00:01:21.479
Earlier, we talked about a technique that
we called activation maximization, which was

00:01:21.479 --> 00:01:26.920
about trying to find an input that makes a
given neuron as excited as possible.

00:01:26.920 --> 00:01:31.929
Here you can see what several individual neurons
have learned, when I trained them to recognize

00:01:31.929 --> 00:01:33.380
wooden patterns.

00:01:33.380 --> 00:01:38.999
In this first layer, it is looking for colors,
then in the second layer, some basic patterns

00:01:38.999 --> 00:01:40.039
emerge.

00:01:40.039 --> 00:01:45.789
As we look into the third layer, we see that
it starts to recognize horizontal, vertical

00:01:45.789 --> 00:01:50.909
and diagonal patterns, and in the fourth and
fifth layers, it uses combinations of the

00:01:50.909 --> 00:01:57.369
previously seen features, and as you can see,
beautiful, somewhat symmetric figures emerge.

00:01:57.369 --> 00:02:01.069
If you would like to see more on this, I put
a link to a previous episode in the video

00:02:01.069 --> 00:02:02.069
description.

00:02:02.069 --> 00:02:08.039
Then, a followup work came for multifaceted
neuron visualizations, that unveiled even

00:02:08.039 --> 00:02:13.530
more beautiful and relevant visualizations,
a good example was showing which neuron is

00:02:13.530 --> 00:02:15.950
responsible for recognizing groceries.

00:02:15.950 --> 00:02:22.120
A new Distill article on this topic has recently
appeared by Christopher Olah and his colleagues

00:02:22.120 --> 00:02:23.120
at Google.

00:02:23.120 --> 00:02:28.890
Distill is a journal that is about publishing
clear explanations to common interesting phenomena

00:02:28.890 --> 00:02:30.689
in machine learning research.

00:02:30.689 --> 00:02:36.010
All their articles so far are beyond amazing,
so make sure to have a look at this new journal

00:02:36.010 --> 00:02:39.739
as a whole, as always, the link is available
in the video description.

00:02:39.739 --> 00:02:43.760
They usually include some web demos that you
can also play with, I'll show you one in a

00:02:43.760 --> 00:02:44.760
moment.

00:02:44.760 --> 00:02:50.530
This article gives a nice rundown of recent
works in optimization-based feature visualization.

00:02:50.530 --> 00:02:55.090
The optimization part can take place in a
number different ways, but it generally means

00:02:55.090 --> 00:03:00.030
that we start out with a noisy image, and
look to change this image to maximize the

00:03:00.030 --> 00:03:02.689
activation of a particular neuron.

00:03:02.689 --> 00:03:07.959
This means that we slowly morph this piece
of noise into an image that provides us information

00:03:07.959 --> 00:03:10.040
on what the network has learned.

00:03:10.040 --> 00:03:15.909
It is indeed a powerful way to perform visualization,
often more informative than just choosing

00:03:15.909 --> 00:03:19.980
the most exciting images for a neuron from
the training database.

00:03:19.980 --> 00:03:25.439
It unveils exactly the information the neuron
is looking for, not something that only correlates

00:03:25.439 --> 00:03:26.439
with that information.

00:03:26.439 --> 00:03:32.349
There is more about not only visualizing the
neurons in isolation, but getting a more detailed

00:03:32.349 --> 00:03:35.939
understanding of the interactions between
these neurons.

00:03:35.939 --> 00:03:41.269
After all, a neural network produces an output
as a combination of these neuron activations,

00:03:41.269 --> 00:03:45.590
so we might as well try to get a detailed
look at how they interact.

00:03:45.590 --> 00:03:50.870
Different regularization techniques to guide
the visualization process towards more informative

00:03:50.870 --> 00:03:53.030
results are also discussed.

00:03:53.030 --> 00:03:58.389
You can also play with some of these web demos,
for instance, this one shows the neuron activations

00:03:58.389 --> 00:04:00.260
with respect to the learning rates.

00:04:00.260 --> 00:04:04.909
There is so much more in the article, I urge
you to read the whole thing, it doesn't take

00:04:04.909 --> 00:04:10.220
that long and it is a wondrous adventure into
the imagination of neural networks.

00:04:10.220 --> 00:04:11.939
How cool is that?

